.TH CCOL_SELECT 3 "2026-10-01" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
ccol_select, ccol_select_timed \- wait for readiness on any of several queues or file descriptors (blocking)
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_retval_t ccol_select(size_t *ready_index, size_t n,
                        ccol_selectable *selectables);

ccol_retval_t ccol_select_timed(size_t *ready_index, size_t n,
                        ccol_selectable *selectables, uint64_t timeout_us);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
The call blocks the thread of the caller until at least one of the n
selectables becomes ready, then writes the 0-based index of that
selectable into *ready_index and returns.

ccol_select() never does the receive or the send itself, for any type of
selectable; it only reports readiness.  The caller must then at once make
its own non-blocking receive or send call on the selectable that won:
ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(), ccol_dynmq_try_recv_zc(),
ccol_circq_try_send_zc() or ccol_dynmq_send_zc() for a queue selectable,
and read(2), recv(2), write(2) or send(2) on selectables[*ready_index].fd
for an fd selectable.  A concurrent consumer or producer can win the race
between the return of ccol_select() and that call, which is the same
time-of-check to time-of-use contract that POSIX select(2) has, so the
call must be non-blocking and the caller must check its result.

ccol_select_timed() takes one more argument, timeout_us.  A value of
UINT64_MAX behaves in the same way as ccol_select(), which waits forever,
and a value of 0 makes a poll that does not block.  Any other value is the
maximum number of microseconds to wait before the call returns
ccol_timed_out.  A value too large to form a deadline saturates to the
latest deadline that the clock can hold, and never wraps into the past.
The library computes the deadline once, before the loop, from
CLOCK_MONOTONIC, so a change of the system time cannot extend the
timeout.

ccol_select() is the same as ccol_select_timed(ready_index, n,
selectables, UINT64_MAX).

A ccol_selectable holds a ccol_circular_queue, a ccol_dynamic_queue, or a
raw file descriptor; build one with ccol_selectable_from_circq(),
ccol_selectable_from_dynq(), ccol_selectable_from_chan(), or
ccol_selectable_from_fd().  A channel resolves to the circular queue below
it before it comes here, so ccol_select() treats a channel exactly as a
plain circular queue.

When there is at least one fd selectable, the call watches the file
descriptors with an epoll(7) instance on Linux, or a kqueue(2) on the
BSDs, which it creates with close-on-exec and closes before it returns.
The call watches each fd for the full call, in level-triggered mode, in
the direction of its selectable, and never changes or closes the fd
itself.  A call that has only queue selectables creates no file
descriptor.

The call allocates a small quantity of memory for each selectable and
frees all of it on every return path.  Any number of threads can call
ccol_select() on the same queue at the same time.

A producer wakes exactly one waiter for each message that it sends or each
slot that it frees, never all of them at once.  A caller that stops
watching a queue while that queue is still ready for its direction
forwards the wake to the next waiter, so the notification that it got is
never absorbed.  This holds for every way of leaving the call: another
selectable becomes ready, the deadline elapses, an fd fires, or the call
returns an error.  No waiter stays parked on a queue that has something
for it.

The call checks each selectable at least one time before it waits, so it
reports a selectable that is ready at the start, also with a timeout_us of
0.  The call loses no wakeup: a send or a free between that check and the
wait also stops the wait.  A spurious wakeup or a signal does not stop the
wait early, and does not make the deadline later.  On the fd path, the
call rounds the remaining time up to whole milliseconds, so the wait never
stops before the deadline; after the deadline, the call checks the file
descriptors one last time and then gives ccol_timed_out.  When the wait
itself fails, the call gives ccol_unexpected_failure.

The call takes its locks in one fixed order, so it cannot deadlock with a
producer, with a consumer, or with a different ccol_select() on the same
queues.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
ready_index   Pointer to a size_t that gets the 0-based index of the
              selectable that became ready.  It is valid only when the
              call returns ccol_success.
n             Number of elements in the selectables array.  It must be
              >= 1 and no larger than INT_MAX, and the call rejects any
              other value with ccol_invalid_args (see RETURN VALUE).  It
              must also be no larger than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(internal
              waiter node); no realistic caller can reach either
              ceiling.
selectables   Array of n ccol_selectable values to watch.
timeout_us    For ccol_select_timed only.  Maximum microseconds to wait.
              UINT64_MAX waits forever, the same as ccol_select.  0
              makes a poll that does not block but reports every
              selectable that is ready at the moment of the call.  Any
              other value waits at least that many microseconds before
              it reports a timeout.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success            A selectable is ready and *ready_index is set.
                        The caller must then make its own receive or
                        send call (see DESCRIPTION).

ccol_timed_out          For ccol_select_timed only.  timeout_us elapsed
                        before any selectable became ready.
                        *ready_index does not change.

ccol_invalid_args       One of these holds: ready_index or selectables
                        is NULL; n is 0; n is larger than INT_MAX,
                        because the library narrows the index of a
                        matched selectable through a plain int; n is
                        larger than SIZE_MAX / sizeof(internal waiter
                        node), because the internal waiter-node array,
                        which has one node for each selectable, would
                        overflow size_t (no n that a caller can
                        realistically build reaches either of those two
                        ceilings); a queue selectable holds a NULL queue
                        pointer; an fd selectable holds a negative fd;
                        or a type tag is present that the library does
                        not know.
ccol_not_enough_memory  malloc failed to allocate the internal waiter
                        node array, which has one node for each
                        selectable.
ccol_unexpected_failure epoll_create1(2), eventfd(2), or epoll_ctl(2)
                        failed with an errno that the library does not
                        expect.  Both functions can return this, but
                        only when at least one fd selectable is present.
                        ccol_select_timed() also returns it in one more
                        case, which needs no fd selectable and a
                        timeout_us other than UINT64_MAX: the internal
                        timed wait on a condition variable fails with an
                        error that is not a timeout.  Treat this as a
                        fatal error in the configuration, for example a
                        full fd table or memory under pressure.  An fd
                        selectable that names a file that epoll(7)
                        cannot watch also gives this code, on every
                        call.  A regular file and a directory are such
                        files: epoll_ctl(2) refuses them with EPERM,
                        where poll(2) and select(2) report them as
                        always ready.  The FreeBSD build also refuses
                        them, although kqueue(2) can watch them, so that
                        one program operates in the same way on the two
                        systems.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The library allocates the waiter nodes on the heap, one for each
selectable, for as long as the call lasts, and frees them on every return
path, error paths included, so a large n never causes an overflow of the
stack.

Any number of threads can call ccol_select() on the same queue at the same
time, because the waiter list is a linked list that grows as needed and
has no fixed capacity.

When several ccol_select() threads watch a queue that becomes ready, the
producer wakes only the head waiter, which is a single wake.  The waiter
that wakes always forwards the notify to the next waiter in line before it
returns, whether or not its own receive or send call succeeds, so every
waiter that is interested gets a chance to observe the same readiness.
This cascade of wakes stops a thundering herd, and also stops the gap in
liveness that would otherwise happen when several messages arrive, or
several slots become free, one after the other in a short time.

A caller can turn writing off on a queue with ccol_circq_disable_sending()
or ccol_dynmq_disable_sending().  Read-direction waiters on that queue
stay registered and keep waiting, and ccol_select() unblocks once sending
is on again and a message arrives.  The library wakes write-direction
waiters at once, so they can observe the new state.

Every file descriptor must stay open for the whole ccol_select() call;
closing an fd that a running ccol_select() watches is undefined
behaviour.

The same rule applies to a queue or a ccol_channel that goes to
ccol_selectable_from_circq(), ccol_selectable_from_dynq() or
ccol_selectable_from_chan(): nothing may destroy it while a ccol_select()
or ccol_select_timed() call watches it.  ccol_circular_queue_destroy(),
ccol_dynamic_queue_destroy() and ccol_channel_destroy() assert when the
queue has a linked waiter, from a ccol_select() or ccol_select_timed()
call that is in progress or from a live ccol_event_loop registration.
Without that assert, the waiter node would stay linked and point to the
mutex of the queue, which the destroy is about to free.

The same fd can appear in more than one selectable inside one call, with
the directions mixed in any way; an exact duplicate of the same direction
twice is allowed too.  One example is a watch on one connected socket for
readability and writability at once.  The library combines selectables
that share an fd into one registration below.  When that registration
fires, ccol_select() resolves the index that it gives back by walking the
selectables that share the fd in the order that the caller gave them, and
picks the first one whose own direction is really satisfied by what
became ready.

The library uses POSIX mutexes and condition variables inside, plus
epoll(7) and eventfd(2) on Linux, or kqueue(2) and eventfd(2) on FreeBSD,
when there is an fd selectable.  This page gives the names of the
epoll(7) calls of the Linux build; the FreeBSD build operates in the same
way.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
/* Fan-in from two circular queues and a ccol_channel: */
size_t idx;
ccol_retval_t r = ccol_select(&idx, 3,
    (ccol_selectable[]){
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read),
    });
if (r == ccol_success) {
    printf("selectable %zu is ready\en", idx);
}

/* Equivalent using the convenience macro: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q1, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_chan(ch, ccol_select_read));

/* Mixed read+write: wake when q0 has room OR dq has a message: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_write),
        ccol_selectable_from_dynq(dq,  ccol_select_read));

/* Fan-in from a queue and a pipe read end: */
int pfd[2];
if (pipe(pfd) != 0) { perror("pipe"); exit(1); }
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_fd(pfd[0], ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_success) {
    c_message_t msg;
    if (idx == 0) {
        /* queue is ready; claim it ourselves (zero-copy) */
        if (ccol_circq_try_recv_zc(q0, &msg) == ccol_success) {
            printf("queue message size: %zu\en", msg.size);
            free(msg.data);
        }
    } else {
        /* fd is ready; read it ourselves */
        char buf[512];
        ssize_t n = read(pfd[0], buf, sizeof(buf));
        printf("read %zd bytes from fd\en", n);
    }
}

/* Timed wait with 500 ms deadline: */
r = ccol_select_timed_va(&idx, 500000,
        ccol_selectable_from_circq(q0, ccol_select_read));
if (r == ccol_timed_out) {
    printf("no source ready within 500 ms\en");
}

/* Watch one connected socket for either direction, whichever comes first: */
r = ccol_select_va(&idx,
        ccol_selectable_from_fd(sock_fd, ccol_select_read),
        ccol_selectable_from_fd(sock_fd, ccol_select_write));
if (r == ccol_success) {
    if (idx == 0) {
        char buf[512];
        ssize_t n = read(sock_fd, buf, sizeof(buf));
        printf("read %zd bytes\en", n);
    } else if (write(sock_fd, "hi", 2) < 0) {
        perror("write");  /* EAGAIN: a different writer used the room */
    }
}
.fi
